  <subsubsection xml:id="ai_quizzes_exams">
    <title>Quizzes and Exams</title>
      <p>
	The use of <init>AI</init> tools, or of any electronic device that can access them, is <alert>prohibited</alert> during all quizzes and exams.
	Any such use will be treated as a case of academic dishonesty under the <xref ref="academic_honor_code" text="title"/>.
      </p>
  </subsubsection>
  <subsubsection xml:id="ai_homework_studying">
    <title>Homework and Studying</title>
    <p>
      Homework is where you learn to write proofs, and the same standard applies to <init>AI</init> that applies to working with classmates: whatever help you use, you must be able to reproduce every solution on your own.
      Within that standard, <init>AI</init> tools are permitted.
      Productive uses include
      <ul>
	<li>
	  asking for a different explanation of a definition or theorem,
	</li>
	<li>generating extra practice problems,</li>
	<li>asking it to find the gaps in a proof you have already written.</li>
      </ul>
      However, asking it to write the proof of an assigned exercise before you have made a serious attempt yourself is generally <alert>not productive</alert>.
      Since the quizzes are drawn from exactly those problems, this usage is the one most likely to affect your grade.
    </p>

    <warning xml:id="ai_warning">
      <p>
	Reading and writing proofs is an art form that improves only with sustained practice.
	You are in this course precisely because that skill is still developing, and outsourcing the writing to <init>AI</init> removes the very practice that would develop it.
      </p>
      <p>
	The most critical component of that skill is judging whether an argument is logically valid, and while you are learning you <alert>will</alert> sometimes get that judgment wrong.
	<init>AI</init> tools frequently produce confidently wrong proofs.
	Until you can reliably discern between valid and invalid proof, you <alert>will</alert> struggle to understand whether the tool has helped you or misled you.
	Understanding the methods of proof yourself is therefore a prerequisite for using these tools effectively, not something they can replace.
      </p>
    </warning>
    <!-- <p> -->
    <!--   If you are ever unsure whether a particular use of <init>AI</init> is appropriate, ask me <alert>before</alert> using it. -->
    <!-- </p> -->
  </subsubsection>
</subsection>
